    "case_id": "703",
    "case_summary": "A 64-year-old woman with a 5-year history of diabetes mellitus, on maintenance dialysis for diabetic nephropathy for 2 years. Medical history included hypertension and breast cancer. One month prior to presentation, she experienced vomiting and diarrhea that resolved after approximately 1 week. Early in the presenting month, she slowly developed difficulty in moving extremities, low-volume voice, and slow speech. Symptoms progressed gradually, leading to difficulty in getting up or walking steadily.\n\nBlood pressure: 163/76 mmHg. Neurological examination revealed:- No disturbance of consciousness- Mask-like face- Hypophonia- Bradykinesia- Rigidity of both upper limbs- Left terminal tremor- Weakness of proximal muscles in both lower limbs- Gait disturbance (short and shuffling steps, reduced arm swing)- Positive Babinski and Chaddock signs in left lower extremity\n\n- Laboratory Tests:  * Blood tests showed no abnormalities  * Normal vitamin B1, thyroid function, and lactate levels  * Normal blood gas analysis without acidosis  * Normal spinal fluid test- Imaging Studies:  1. Brain MRI:     Title: T2-weighted brain MRI     Description: High-intensity areas in bilateral lenticular nucleus with lentiform fork sign    2. Diffusion-weighted imaging:     Description: Equal intensity    3. Dopamine transporter scan:     Description: No decreased accumulation, showed increased levels in the lesions    4. Magnetic resonance spectroscopy (MRS):     Description: Demonstrated a lactate peak    5. Arterial spin labeling (ASL):     Description: Showed hyperperfusion in the lesions",
